<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">
	<PropertyGroup>
		<OutputType>Exe</OutputType>
		<TargetFramework>net10.0-android</TargetFramework>
		<SupportedOSPlatformVersion>23</SupportedOSPlatformVersion>
		<Nullable>enable</Nullable>
		<ApplicationId>com.bigmissionmotorsports.redmist</ApplicationId>
		<ApplicationVersion>$(SharedVersion)</ApplicationVersion>
		<ApplicationDisplayVersion>$(SharedVersion)</ApplicationDisplayVersion>
		<AndroidUseAapt2>True</AndroidUseAapt2>
		<AndroidCreatePackagePerAbi>False</AndroidCreatePackagePerAbi>
		<AndroidPackageFormat>aab</AndroidPackageFormat>
		<UserSecretsId>8c31261b-5551-40d9-a50a-bfb280d7f018</UserSecretsId>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- Ahead-of-time compilation, measured on a Samsung SM-S135DL (Android 13, arm64):
	     median time to first frame over 7 launches each, via `am start -W`.

	         off (as shipped)   10063 ms
	         profiled AOT        9242 ms   -8%
	         full AOT            5922 ms   -41%

	     Unprofiled deliberately. AndroidEnableProfiledAot restricts compilation to the
	     methods in dotnet.aotprofile, which covers .NET Android's own startup path and knows
	     nothing about Avalonia - hence the 8%. Compiling everything is what actually pays, at
	     14.4 MB more per-ABI native code (16.5 -> 30.9 MB compressed, arm64), which is what a
	     Play split delivers, and about 20 MB more PSS.

	     Release only, and the condition is load-bearing. Nothing in the SDK gates the AOT
	     compile on configuration - _AndroidAot runs on AotAssemblies alone - so setting these
	     unconditionally puts the whole per-ABI compile into every debug deploy too.

	     Neither line is redundant, though not for the obvious reason. RunAOTCompilation is
	     already true by default for a Release MonoVM build, in
	     Microsoft.Android.Sdk.DefaultProperties.targets; what this replaces is the explicit
	     false that used to opt out of it. AndroidEnableProfiledAot does have to be set here,
	     because it defaults to true once AOT is on.

	     Costs a long build and needs the Android NDK, which CI runners have preinstalled but a
	     dev machine may not.

	     One of six device runs died with a SIGSEGV whose stack is entirely inside libmonosgen.
	     That run's crash (tools/device/results/smoke/20260831T030142Z.json, pid 11820, fault
	     addr 0x8000) is the GC bridge crash described below, on the AOT build. The phone has the
	     same crash on Play builds back to its oldest tombstone, October 2025, so AOT did not
	     introduce it; whether AOT changes how often it happens is unmeasured. -->
	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release'">
		<AndroidEnableProfiledAot>false</AndroidEnableProfiledAot>
		<RunAOTCompilation>true</RunAOTCompilation>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- Mono's major collector runs non-concurrently: MONO_GC_PARAMS=major=marksweep, where the
	     SDK default is marksweep-conc. Debug builds too, so development runs the collector that
	     ships.

	     This is for the app's recurring native crash - a SIGSEGV inside libmonosgen with no
	     managed frames, probably among the events in Sentry REDMIST-APP-M, which carry no native
	     stack. The test phone holds ten tombstones from the app, October 2025 to August 2026 over
	     six runtime builds. Symbolized against Microsoft's symbols for each build, they are:

	         7  the Tarjan GC bridge in a nursery collection, following a reference somewhere in
	            the graph reachable from Java peers that holds garbage - 0x37, 0x2020, 0x8000,
	            UTF-16 text - some of them faulting on the object header itself:
	            bridge_object_forward or is_opaque_object < push_object < dfs
	            < processing_stw_step < finish_gray_stack < collect_nursery
	         1  an abort in the concurrent collector's card scan of a large object, "How can an
	            object and a reference inside it not be in the same block?" (October 2025)
	         2  a bad class pointer in mono_rgctx_lazy_fetch_trampoline, which this setting is
	            not known to affect

	     The bridge crash is dotnet/runtime#123495. The Mono GC maintainer closed it into
	     #123537, a SIGSEGV in the concurrent mark, as another face of the same problem; as of
	     September 2026 that issue is open against 12.0.0. His first suggestion there is this
	     setting, and the reporter's first test "looks very promising... couldn't reproduce the
	     crash", with no follow-up. Neither Mono GC change .NET 10 has taken stopped it - a
	     bridge change merged just before 10.0.0, and #125213's March 2026 fix to card scanning
	     of large objects, which may account for the one abort: runtime 10.0.11 still crashed in
	     August 2026. What he points to instead is .NET 11, where Android defaults to CoreCLR;
	     drop this when the app moves there.

	     What it changes: a major collection marks the whole heap with the app stopped instead of
	     concurrently, so nursery collections no longer run in the middle of a concurrent mark.
	     That interleaving is the suspect, not a confirmed cause. The cost is longer major
	     pauses. Judge it by native crashes per release across issues, or by crash-free sessions,
	     not by REDMIST-APP-M alone: once tombstone capture (MainActivity.cs) names the frames, the
	     same crash groups under its real stack rather than that issue's single unknown frame, and
	     the first launch with tombstones on also reports each phone's last crash from before.

	     Measured on the SM-S135DL, a Release build of each, 2026-09-14: smoke and soak pass with
	     nothing beyond run-to-run noise - cold start 6059 ms against 6138, soak PSS 314 -> 368 MB
	     against 312 -> 370. Frame cost was read from Choreographer's skipped-frame warnings, which
	     on that phone report only skips of 30 frames or more, because neither Looper's
	     slow-message log (refused to shell) nor SGen's per-collection timing (silent even with
	     debug.mono.log=gc) could be read. Both builds freeze the live screen for about 600 ms at
	     a time, each freeze followed within a fifth of a second by an explicit ART collection of
	     the kind the GC bridge asks for, which points to Mono collections handing dead Java peers
	     to the bridge; 35% of those collections froze the screen on the concurrent build and 44%
	     on this one. Against a simulated live feed, five runs of the concurrent build had 3.0
	     freezes per 100 hub messages and three runs of this one had 3.7. Runs of one build spread
	     about as widely, but in the one session that alternated builds, both runs of this one were
	     worse than all three of the other. Eight runs on one phone cannot settle it: treat a rise
	     of up to a third as possible. One soak each, 4 freezes against 6, is too few to call.

	     To check it took effect, look for major=marksweep in
	     obj/<Configuration>/net10.0-android/android/environment.<abi>.ll - not for a missing
	     thread: concurrent sweep stays on, so an SGen worker thread still exists.

	     Keep it a property. If any AndroidEnvironment file, including one a NuGet package brings
	     in, sets MONO_GC_PARAMS, the SDK drops this value entirely, so such a file has to carry
	     major=marksweep itself. -->
	<PropertyGroup>
		<AndroidEnableSGenConcurrent>false</AndroidEnableSGenConcurrent>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<!-- Android has to keep its JIT, and this is what holds it to that.

	     SignalR's MessagePack hub protocol writes every outbound argument through
	     MessagePackSerializer's non-generic entry point, which takes a ref MessagePackWriter.
	     MessagePack gates that path on RuntimeFeature.IsDynamicCodeSupported: with dynamic code
	     it binds a delegate over a MakeGenericMethod'd MethodInfo, and without it there is
	     nothing to bind instead, because a ref struct cannot be boxed into the object[] that
	     MethodInfo.Invoke needs - which is how every other non-generic overload degrades. So it
	     throws NotSupportedException (MessagePack 3.1.8, MessagePackSerializer.NonGenerics).
	     Reported as dotnet/aspnetcore#34585.

	     iOS does not reach any of this, because BigMission.Shared's TryAddMessagePack puts the
	     hub on JSON there - RuntimeInformation.IsOSPlatform("IOS"), not a compile-time switch,
	     so grepping this repo for it finds nothing. Forcing MessagePack on an iPhone is how the
	     failure was found, and the shape of it is why this is a build error rather than a
	     comment: the hub connects, and the first invoke fails inside the protocol - below every
	     call site, so TryInvokeAsync never sees it to log it - leaving the app on 5-second REST
	     polling. No crash and no error, and until SignalR's own logging was forwarded to the
	     app's factory the only symptom was timing that had quietly gone stale.

	     None of it applies today, and that was read off the build rather than assumed: Release
	     with RunAOTCompilation gives AndroidAotMode=Normal, which keeps the JIT, and the
	     runtimeconfig switch RuntimeFeature.IsDynamicCodeSupported is written true. The .NET
	     SDK's trimming defaults set DynamicCodeSupport=true and the Android SDK never touches
	     it; the iOS SDK is the one that overrides it to false. Debug builds come out as
	     AndroidAotMode=Interpreter and write no switch at all, which is the configuration the
	     app has been developed against all along.

	     The mode check is an allow-list of the three this repo actually builds. Full and the
	     LLVMOnly variants are the ones to watch - they are Mono's no-JIT modes, the same shape
	     as iOS - but listing rather than excluding is the point: anything new stops here and
	     gets checked. DynamicCodeSupport would not catch them on its own, because
	     AndroidAotMode=Full leaves it true - the runtimeconfig would go on claiming a JIT the
	     runtime no longer has. -->
	<Target Name="VerifyAndroidKeepsItsJit" BeforeTargets="Build">
		<Error Condition="'$(AndroidAotMode)' != 'None' And '$(AndroidAotMode)' != 'Normal' And '$(AndroidAotMode)' != 'Interpreter'"
		       Text="AndroidAotMode is '$(AndroidAotMode)'. Only None, Normal and Interpreter are known to keep the JIT, which SignalR's MessagePack hub protocol needs: without it the hub connects, the first invoke throws NotSupportedException, and the app degrades to REST polling with no error. Confirm the mode keeps dynamic code before allowing it here." />
		<Error Condition="'$(DynamicCodeSupport)' == 'false'"
		       Text="DynamicCodeSupport is false, so RuntimeFeature.IsDynamicCodeSupported is false at runtime and SignalR's MessagePack hub protocol throws on its first invoke - silently, degrading the app to REST polling. Leave it unset, or move the hub to JSON as iOS does." />
		<Error Condition="'$(PublishAot)' == 'true'"
		       Text="PublishAot puts the Android head on NativeAOT, which has no JIT. SignalR's MessagePack hub protocol throws on its first invoke and the app degrades to REST polling with no error." />
	</Target>

	<!-- Debug symbol upload. Turns itself on only when CI supplies SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN, so local and
	     PR builds are untouched. sentry-cli reads the token, org and project from the environment
	     rather than MSBuild properties, keeping the token off the command line. A bad token or an
	     unreachable server downgrades to a build warning and skips the upload, so a Sentry outage
	     cannot fail a release. -->
	<PropertyGroup Condition="'$(Configuration)' == 'Release' and '$(SENTRY_AUTH_TOKEN)' != ''">
		<SentryUploadSymbols>true</SentryUploadSymbols>
		<!-- Symbols only. SentryUploadSources would also bundle and upload the source itself, which
		     is not needed for readable frames and is a disclosure decision rather than a build one. -->
		<SentryUploadSources>false</SentryUploadSources>
	</PropertyGroup>

	<ItemGroup>
		<PackageReference Include="Avalonia.Android" />
		<PackageReference Include="Xamarin.AndroidX.Core.SplashScreen" />

		<!-- Explicit on the Android head so the android-specific Sentry assets (the Android SDK
		     bindings that capture native crashes and tombstones) are unambiguously selected. -->
		<PackageReference Include="Sentry" />

		<!-- Add explicit SkiaSharp references for Android 16KB page size requirement -->
		<PackageReference Include="SkiaSharp" />
		<PackageReference Include="SkiaSharp.HarfBuzz" />

		<!-- Explicit AndroidX Lifecycle package versions to resolve dependency conflicts -->
		<PackageReference Include="Xamarin.AndroidX.Lifecycle.ViewModelSavedState" />
		<PackageReference Include="Xamarin.AndroidX.Lifecycle.Common" />
		<PackageReference Include="Xamarin.AndroidX.Lifecycle.Common.Java8" />
		<PackageReference Include="Xamarin.AndroidX.Lifecycle.Process" />

		<!-- AndroidX Activity for modern back press handling -->
		<PackageReference Include="Xamarin.AndroidX.Activity" />

		<!-- Exclude JVM variant of Compose Runtime Annotation to prevent duplicate types -->
		<PackageReference Include="Xamarin.AndroidX.Compose.Runtime.Annotation.Jvm" ExcludeAssets="all" />

	</ItemGroup>

	<!-- RedMist.TimingCommon is deliberately NOT marked trimmable, and this comment is here so
	     that nobody adds it back. iOS carries <TrimmableAssembly Include="RedMist.TimingCommon" />
	     and it is tempting to mirror.

	     What used to make it tempting is gone. TimingCommon 1.17.2 stopped compiling its TypeScript
	     generation specs into the shipped assembly, so there is no longer a TypeGen reference for
	     the trimmer to clean up after, and no spec classes whose removal reclaimed IL. Trimming
	     here would now buy essentially nothing.

	     It also breaks live timing outright. From 1.17.1 the patch types carry
	     [MessagePackFormatter(typeof(SessionStatePatchFormatter))], and that attribute has no
	     DynamicallyAccessedMembers on it. The trimmer keeps the formatter type, because typeof
	     names it, and then removes everything inside - the interface it implements, its Instance
	     field, Serialize and Deserialize. What ships is an empty class that the attribute still
	     points at, so AttributeFormatterResolver finds it, cannot activate it, and every patch
	     fails to deserialize. Measured on the phone: session patches went from eleven in ten
	     seconds to zero, with the hub still connected, and logcat carrying

	         The RedMist.TimingCommon.Models.SessionStatePatchFormatter formatter has no default
	         constructor nor implements the singleton pattern.

	     Nothing in the build names it. Trim analysis warnings are suppressed here by the Android
	     SDK default, which turns them off whenever TrimMode is not full, and turning them back on
	     produces 357 IL2 warnings that between them say only that MessagePack does not support
	     trimming - none points at a formatter. The app meanwhile goes on rendering its last state.

	     iOS carried the same line and got away with it, because its hub runs JSON and never asks
	     MessagePack for these types - it was shipping the same hollow formatters, unnoticed. It no
	     longer carries it: measured against ios-arm64 on a Mac, the line emptied both formatters of
	     their methods, fields and interfaces, and removing it cost about 45 KB on a 39 MB IPA. So
	     neither head trims this assembly now, for the same reason.

	     A descriptor here would work, and the reason not to add one is the payoff rather than the
	     mechanism: ILLink descriptors take wildcards, so
	     <type fullname="RedMist.TimingCommon.Models.*PatchFormatter" preserve="all" /> keeps every
	     formatter the generator will ever emit, verified by relinking. What it buys is a little IL -
	     and f93794f already measured that less IL here does not mean a smaller app, because that
	     build's APK came out 197 KB larger. Not worth a mechanism that has to stay correct. The fix
	     that would be worth having is upstream, on MessagePack's
	     MessagePackFormatterAttribute, whose formatterType parameter wants
	     [DynamicallyAccessedMembers(PublicConstructors | PublicFields)]; failing that, the patch
	     generator emitting a DynamicDependency alongside each formatter. -->
	<ItemGroup>
		<ProjectReference Include="..\RedMist.Timing.UI\RedMist.Timing.UI.csproj" />
	</ItemGroup>
</Project>
